<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otto Wille Kuusinen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1881">1881</a>–<a href="/years/1964">1964</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Otto Wille Kuusinen was a Finnish communist who fled to Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnish-civil-war">Finnish Civil War</a> of 1918 and became a leading figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a> of 1939 Stalin made him head of the puppet Finnish Democratic Republic at Terijoki. He then chaired the Supreme Soviet of the Karelo-Finnish SSR and later served in the Soviet Presidium.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otto-wille-kuusinen.jpg" alt="Otto Wille Kuusinen" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otto_Wille_Kuusinen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Otto Wilhelm Kuusinen was born on 4 October 1881 at Laukaa in the Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, the son of a village tailor. His mother died when he was two years old, and the family moved to Jyväskylä. He graduated from the Jyväskylä Lyceum in 1900 and entered the University of Helsinki, studying philosophy, aesthetics and art history, and took his degree as a candidate of philosophy in 1902. As a student he was interested in Fennoman conservatism and Alkioism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1906 Kuusinen toppled the more moderate party chairman J. K. Kari and came to dominate the Social Democratic Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, of which he was chairman from 1911 to 1917. He sat in parliament in 1908 to 1910, 1911 to 1913 and 1916 to 1918. He was a leader of the January 1918 revolution that created the short-lived Finnish Socialist Workers' Republic, in which he was People's Commissar of Education. After the Reds were defeated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnish-civil-war">Finnish Civil War</a> he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and helped form the Finnish Communist Party. He became a prominent official of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a> and a leader in Soviet military intelligence, where he set up a network against the Scandinavian countries.</p><p>Finnish emigrants who went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> to build socialism suffered heavily in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a>. Kuusinen's reputation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> was damaged because he was one of the very few not targeted by show trials, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> and executions. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> advanced in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a> on 30 November 1939, he was pronounced head of the Finnish Democratic Republic, or Terijoki Government, a puppet regime through which Stalin meant to turn Finland into a socialist state. A treaty with the Soviet Union was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 2 December 1939 by Molotov and Kuusinen. The war went otherwise than planned, and the Soviet leadership negotiated peace with Finland's legitimate government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the Winter War Kuusinen chaired the presidium of the Supreme Soviet of the Karelo-Finnish SSR from 1940 to 1956. He was a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> in 1952 to 1953 and from 1957 to 1964, and a secretary of the Central Committee from 1957, and he kept his place under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a>'s de-Stalinization. He was an editor of the textbook Fundamentals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism-Leninism</a> and in 1958 was elected to the Soviet Academy of Sciences. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 17 May 1964, aged 82, and his ashes were buried in the Kremlin Wall Necropolis. His 1926 report on work among the masses spoke of a solar system of organisations around the Communist Party.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Otto Kuusinen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The next day it announced the formation of the Finnish Democratic Republic, nominally based in the border town of Terijoki, with the exiled Finnish communist <b>Otto Wille Kuusinen</b> as head.</blockquote><a href="/state/finnish-democratic-republic">Finnish Democratic Republic</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Otto Wille Kuusinen</b>, former head of the Finnish Democratic Republic, became its head of state.</blockquote><a href="/state/karelo-finnish-soviet-socialist-republic">Karelo-Finnish Soviet Socialist Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Laukaa, in what is now <a href="/countries/finland">Finland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=62.41,25.95&amp;year=1934" aria-label="Laukaa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5080 95 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Otto Wille Kuusinen"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5530 365h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5530 365h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=62.41,25.95&amp;year=1934">What was here in 1934? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
